      "type": "Incorrect asset receiver in fast redeem enabling draining of composer-held assets (double payout / balance drain)",
      "severity": "high",
      "vulnerable_function": "_fastRedeem",
      "location": "wiTryVaultComposer._fastRedeem",
      "explanation": "In `_fastRedeem`, the contract calls `IStakediTryCrosschain(VAULT).fastRedeemThroughComposer(_shareAmount, redeemer, redeemer)`. Under standard ERC4626 semantics, `redeem/withdraw` transfers the redeemed assets to the provided `receiver` (here: `redeemer`), not to the composer. Immediately after, the composer unconditionally calls `_send(ASSET_OFT, _sendParam, _refundAddress)` to send `assets` cross-chain, which requires the composer itself to hold `assets` of `ASSET_OFT` to debit.\n\nIf the vault indeed transfers assets to `redeemer`, the composer is paying the cross-chain leg out of its *own* `ASSET_OFT` balance (if any), effectively creating a second payout for the same redeem. Any `ASSET_OFT` accidentally/mistakenly held by the composer (dust, refunds, transfers, failed flows) becomes stealable by anyone who can trigger FAST_REDEEM with any nonzero share amount.\n\nEven if the composer balance is usually zero, this is still exploitable whenever it is nonzero, and it also risks DoS (revert) if balance is insufficient.",
      "attack_scenario": "1) Composer contract has a positive `ASSET_OFT` balance (e.g., someone mistakenly transfers iTRY/OFT to it, dust accumulates, or a prior operation leaves tokens stranded).\n2) Attacker sends `SHARE_OFT` to the composer with compose command `FAST_REDEEM` and valid `sendParam` pointing to attacker on a destination chain.\n3) Vault fast-redeems and transfers `assets` to attacker on the hub chain because `receiver = redeemer`.\n4) Composer then sends `assets` cross-chain to attacker as well, debiting its own `ASSET_OFT` balance.\n5) Attacker receives value twice; the second leg drains the composer’s held `ASSET_OFT`.",
      "suggested_fix": "Change the fast redeem receiver to the composer so it actually receives the assets it is about to bridge:\n- Call `fastRedeemThroughComposer(_shareAmount, redeemer, address(this))` (or equivalent) and then `_send`.\nAdditionally:\n- Assert post-condition `IERC20(ASSET_OFT).balanceOf(address(this)) >= assets` before `_send`.\n- Consider sending directly from the vault to the OFT/adapter if supported, to avoid custody ambiguity."
    }
  ],
  "overall_explanation": "Main issue found is in `FAST_REDEEM`: the composer appears to instruct the vault to pay redeemed assets to the user, but then also tries to bridge the same amount from the composer’s own balance. If the vault follows typical ERC4626 transfer behavior, this enables draining any `ASSET_OFT` balance held by the composer (and/or causes fast redeem to revert when composer lacks balance). Other reviewed areas (OApp `_lzReceive` routing, refund options header, rescue) did not show a clear, standalone exploit without relying on unseen external contracts."
}
